\title{Canonical Forbidden-Minor Bases from Well-Quasi-Ordering}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Forbidden-minor characterizations convert a hereditary structural property into a finite list of obstructions. We isolate the order-theoretic mechanism behind such characterizations and apply it to matroid minors. Minimal members of any subset of a partial order form an antichain. Under a well-quasi-order hypothesis this antichain is finite, while well-founded descent ensures that every member of the subset lies above a minimal member. Consequently every lower set has a finite canonical forbidden basis, namely the minimal elements of its complement. If a family of matroids is well-quasi-ordered by minors, every minor-closed subclass therefore has finitely many excluded minors, and membership is equivalent to avoiding them. We also prove without any ambient well-quasi-order assumption that the excluded minors of an intersection of two classes lie in the union of the constituent obstruction sets. Algorithms, examples, applications, and the limitations of the conditional result are discussed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Minor relations organize combinatorial objects by simplification. In graphs, deletion removes an edge and contraction identifies its endpoints. Matroid theory abstracts these operations while retaining the dependence structures that make them useful. A class is minor-closed when simplification never takes a member outside the class.

A finite forbidden basis for a class $C$ is a finite family $B$ satisfying
\[
 M\in C \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 \text{no }N\in B\text{ is a minor of }M.
\]
This replaces an unbounded positive description by finitely many minimal failure modes. The purpose of this paper is to separate the general deduction of such a basis from the much harder premise that a particular minor order is a well-quasi-order.

The deduction has three ingredients. Minimal members of a set are incomparable; a well-quasi-order has no infinite antichain; and well-founded descent places a minimal member below every member. Applied to the complement of a lower set, these facts yield a finite canonical obstruction family.

The motivating prospect is a well-quasi-order theorem for matroids representable over a fixed finite field. We do not assert that premise. Instead, we establish exactly what it would imply. We also derive an unconditional closure theorem for intersections of classes already known to have finite canonical bases.

\section{Order-theoretic foundations}

\begin{definition}
Let $(X,\pre)$ be a partial order, and write $y\prec x$ when $y\pre x$ and $y\ne x$. A subset $C\subseteq X$ is a \emph{lower set} if $x\in C$ and $y\pre x$ imply $y\in C$. A subset $A\subseteq X$ is an \emph{antichain} if no two distinct members of $A$ are comparable.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For $U\subseteq X$, its set of minimal members is
\[
 \Min(U)=\{x\in U: \text{for every }y\prec x,\ y\notin U\}.
\]
Minimality is relative to $U$; it does not mean global minimality in $X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Minimal members form an antichain]
For every subset $U$ of a partially ordered set, $\Min(U)$ is an antichain.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Take $x,y\in\Min(U)$ with $x\pre y$. If $x\ne y$, then $x\prec y$. Since $x\in U$, this contradicts the defining minimality of $y$. Hence $x=y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
A partial order is a \emph{well-quasi-order} if every infinite sequence $x_0,x_1,\ldots$ contains indices $i<j$ such that $x_i\pre x_j$.
\end{definition}

For partial orders this condition rules out both infinite strict descent and infinite antichains. The two exclusions have separate functions in the finite-basis argument.

\begin{lemma}[Finiteness of minimal members]
If $(X,\pre)$ is well-quasi-ordered, then $\Min(U)$ is finite for every $U\subseteq X$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The preceding lemma makes $\Min(U)$ an antichain, and every antichain in a well-quasi-order is finite.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[A minimal member below every member]
Let $(X,\pre)$ be well-quasi-ordered. If $x\in U$, then some $b\in\Min(U)$ satisfies $b\pre x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Consider the nonempty set $U_x=\{y\in U:y\pre x\}$. Well-foundedness gives a minimal member $b$ of $U_x$. If $z\in U$ and $z\prec b$, then transitivity gives $z\pre x$, so $z\in U_x$, a contradiction. Thus $b\in\Min(U)$ and $b\pre x$.
\end{proof}

\section{The finite canonical forbidden basis}

For a lower set $C\subseteq X$, define its canonical forbidden basis by
\[
 B_C=\Min(X\setminus C).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Finite Canonical Forbidden-Basis Theorem]
Let $(X,\pre)$ be a well-quasi-ordered partial order and let $C\subseteq X$ be a lower set. Then $B_C$ is a finite antichain and, for every $x\in X$,
\[
 x\in C \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 \forall b\in B_C,\ b\notpre x.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding lemmas give finiteness and the antichain property. If $x\in C$ and $b\in B_C$ satisfied $b\pre x$, lower closure would imply $b\in C$, contradicting $b\in X\setminus C$. Conversely, if $x\notin C$, the minimal-member lemma applied to $X\setminus C$ produces $b\in B_C$ with $b\pre x$. This proves the contrapositive of the reverse implication.
\end{proof}

The basis is canonical because it is determined solely by the class and the order. It is irredundant because no basis member contains another. Removing $b\in B_C$ from the avoidance list causes $b$ itself to pass every remaining test despite lying outside $C$.

Both aspects of well-quasi-ordering are necessary. Without the antichain condition there can be infinitely many minimal outsiders. Without well-foundedness, the complement can be nonempty without having any minimal member; the integers under their usual order with $C=\varnothing$ give a basic example.

\section{Matroid minors}

\begin{definition}
A finite matroid is a pair $M=(E,\mathcal I)$ where $E$ is finite and $\mathcal I\subseteq 2^E$ satisfies: $\varnothing\in\mathcal I$; subsets of independent sets are independent; and if $I,J\in\mathcal I$ with $|I|<|J|$, then some $e\in J\setminus I$ makes $I\cup\{e\}$ independent.
\end{definition}

Deletion and contraction remove ground-set elements in the standard dependence-preserving ways. A matroid $N$ is a \emph{minor} of $M$, written $N\pre_m M$, if it is obtained by finitely many deletions and contractions.

\begin{definition}
A matroid class $C$ is \emph{minor-closed} if $M\in C$ and $N\pre_m M$ imply $N\in C$. A matroid $M$ is an \emph{excluded minor} for $C$ if $M\notin C$ and every strict minor of $M$ belongs to $C$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Excluded minors are minimal outsiders]
For every matroid class $C$, a matroid $M$ is an excluded minor if and only if it is a minimal member of the complement of $C$ under $\pre_m$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Both statements say that $M$ is outside $C$ and no strict minor of $M$ is outside $C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Incomparability]
Distinct excluded minors of any matroid class are incomparable under the minor relation.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional Finite Excluded-Minor Theorem]
Let $\mathcal M$ be a family of matroids well-quasi-ordered by $\pre_m$. If $C\subseteq\mathcal M$ is minor-closed, then its excluded-minor set $B_C$ is finite and
\[
 M\in C \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 \forall N\in B_C,\ N\notpre_m M.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Minor-closedness makes $C$ a lower set, and the preceding proposition identifies excluded minors with the minimal complement. Apply the Finite Canonical Forbidden-Basis Theorem.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Finite-field representability}
A matroid is representable over a finite field $\mathbb F_q$ if its ground-set elements can be assigned vectors over $\mathbb F_q$ so that matroid independence agrees with linear independence. Matroids representable over $\mathbb F_2$ are binary, while those representable over $\mathbb F_3$ are ternary. Graphic matroids are binary, but not every binary matroid is graphic.

A broad well-quasi-order theorem for a family of $\mathbb F_q$-representable matroids would, by the preceding theorem, provide finite excluded-minor descriptions for every minor-closed subclass of that family. The theorem here does not establish that premise for ternary matroids or for arbitrary finite fields. Concrete claims about small matroids also require separate matrix representations or exact nonrepresentability certificates.

\section{Intersections of finitely based classes}

\begin{theorem}[Canonical obstructions to an intersection]
For lower sets $C,D\subseteq X$ in any partial order,
\[
 B_{C\cap D}\subseteq B_C\cup B_D.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $x\in B_{C\cap D}$. Then $x\notin C$ or $x\notin D$. If $x\notin C$, every $y\prec x$ belongs to $C\cap D$ by minimality of $x$ outside the intersection, hence belongs to $C$. Therefore $x\in B_C$. The case $x\notin D$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Finite-basis closure]
If $B_C$ and $B_D$ are finite, then $B_{C\cap D}$ is finite. In particular, if two matroid classes have finitely many excluded minors, their intersection does as well, and every excluded minor of the intersection is an excluded minor of at least one constituent class.
\end{corollary}

No ambient well-quasi-order is used. The inclusion can be strict: an obstruction to one class can contain a smaller obstruction to the other and thereby cease to be minimal for the intersection.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Extracting a basis from finite data}
Suppose a finite universe $X$ is explicit, along with the order and a membership predicate for $C$. Form $U=X\setminus C$. Retain $x\in U$ exactly when no distinct $y\in U$ satisfies $y\pre x$. The retained list is $\Min(U)=B_C$ by definition. For $n=|X|$ and constant-time predicates, direct pairwise comparison takes $O(n^2)$ time and $O(n)$ auxiliary space.

\subsection{Membership from a known basis}
Given a finite basis $B_C$, test $b\pre x$ for every $b\in B_C$. Return nonmembership upon the first success and membership if all tests fail. Correctness is the avoidance equivalence. If the test for $b\pre x$ costs $T_b(|x|)$, total time is
\[
 O\!\left(\sum_{b\in B_C}T_b(|x|)\right).
\]
Finiteness guarantees only finitely many tests; it does not guarantee that each test is efficient.

\subsection{Combining bases}
Given $B_C$ and $B_D$, begin with $B_C\cup B_D$. Remove candidates lying in $C\cap D$, then remove every remaining candidate with a strict predecessor among the remaining outsiders. The result is the canonical basis of the intersection. If $k=|B_C|+|B_D|$, direct minimization requires $O(k^2)$ order comparisons after membership is known.

\section{Examples}

\begin{example}[Subset lattice]
Let $X=2^{\{1,2,3,4\}}$ under inclusion and let $C=\{S:|S|\le2\}$. Then
\[
 B_C=\{\{1,2,3\},\{1,2,4\},\{1,3,4\},\{2,3,4\}\}.
\]
Every forbidden subset contains one of these four triples. Now let $D=\{S:\{1,2\}\nsubseteq S\}$. Then $B_D=\{\{1,2\}\}$, while
\[
 B_{C\cap D}=\{\{1,2\},\{1,3,4\},\{2,3,4\}\}.
\]
The triples containing $\{1,2\}$ are no longer minimal, illustrating that the intersection inclusion may be strict.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Divisibility]
Order the positive integers by divisibility and set
\[
 C=\{n:6\nmid n\text{ and }10\nmid n\}.
\]
The canonical basis is $\{6,10\}$, so membership is exactly avoidance of divisibility by these two incomparable minimal outsiders.
\end{example}

\section{Applications and limitations}
A finite basis is a compact structural classification. Each excluded minor records a distinct minimal failure mode. When minor testing is tractable, it also gives a recognition algorithm and concrete negative certificates consisting of a forbidden minor and a sequence of deletions and contractions.

The intersection theorem makes finite obstruction theories modular: conjunctions of two finitely based minor-closed properties remain finitely based. Iterating the binary result motivates the finite-indexed inclusion
\[
 B_{\bigcap_{i=1}^k C_i}\subseteq\bigcup_{i=1}^k B_{C_i}.
\]

Several limitations are essential. No well-quasi-order theorem is proved here for all ternary matroids or for all matroids representable over a fixed finite field. No matrix model of representability, exhaustive rank-three nine-element census, or classification of named small matroids is supplied. Such work requires exact generation up to isomorphism and exact representability tests. Finally, existence of a finite basis is not an effective size bound or a method for discovering its members.

\section{Future work}
Future work should define matrix representability over $\mathbb F_3$ and prove preservation by deletion and contraction; connect the conditional theorem to a precisely specified finite representable family; construct standard examples from rank functions or matrices; and develop enumeration up to matroid isomorphism before undertaking small-parameter searches. The binary intersection theorem should be extended to finite indexed intersections. Graph--matroid comparisons should also distinguish graphic matroids from the strictly larger class of binary matroids.

The central structural challenge remains the appropriate well-quasi-order theorem. Once such a premise is established, the route to finite canonical obstruction bases is immediate.

\section{Conclusion}
Minimal outsiders form an antichain. Well-quasi-ordering makes that antichain finite, and well-founded descent places one of its members below every outsider. Lower closure then turns avoidance into an exact membership criterion. For matroids, the minimal outsiders are excluded minors; hence every minor-closed class inside a well-quasi-ordered matroid family has a finite, canonical, irredundant obstruction basis. Independently, finite bases survive binary intersections. These results identify the precise finite-classification consequence of any future Robertson--Seymour-style theorem for finite-field-representable matroids while keeping the unproved premise explicit.

\end{document}

